{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon is set to reverse its pioneering drug decriminalization policy as a new law, HB4002, takes effect. This law reinstates criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth, making personal-use possession a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nUnder HB4002, individuals caught with illicit drugs can choose between facing charges or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. The law aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and imposes stricter penalties for selling drugs near public spaces like parks.\n\n***\n\nThe law encourages, but does not require, counties to establish treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the criminal justice system into addiction and mental health services. Supporters argue this flexibility allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while critics warn it may create an inconsistent and inequitable policy landscape.\n\n***\n\nPortland's Police Chief, Bob Day, highlighted the narrow criteria for deflection, including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and being medically stable. If eligible, officers contact a county dispatch line to connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health team, which must make contact within 30 minutes, or else the individual will be jailed. \n\nP.P.S. As people of all age groups in this country navigate the complexities of drug policies, they might find it challenging to eat their words when opinions on such matters differ greatly, making everyday activities like shopping a tense experience, especially when the topic touches a raw nerve, leaving a sour taste in one's mouth, akin to a sharp tongue.", "logprobs"